WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:03.449
Film Courage: Why not pitch Netflix and get
them to do an upcoming project?

00:00:03.449 --> 00:00:06.520
Brad Sykes, Writer/Director: I doubt I would
ever get in the room with them?

00:00:06.520 --> 00:00:08.410
I mean…I’m just being honest.

00:00:08.410 --> 00:00:13.210
I don’t know how I would get in the room
with them?

00:00:13.210 --> 00:00:20.340
It’s weird in this business too and this
is the honest truth is you know I am sure

00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:24.890
and I’m not saying it was easy for The Duffer
Brothers with STRANGER THINGS but it did probably

00:00:24.890 --> 00:00:30.080
help that they hadn’t done a whole lot when
they pitched that.

00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:33.510
What happens in this business is it’s kind
of a double-edged sword if you start to do

00:00:33.510 --> 00:00:38.470
things and you have a track record and you
made X number of movies well okay that’s

00:00:38.470 --> 00:00:42.060
great because it shows you can do this and
you’ve done it more than once and obviously

00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:46.580
you’ve got some kind of track record skills
and so on.

00:00:46.580 --> 00:00:51.469
But for some people depending on what type
of movies you make and in my case it’s horror

00:00:51.469 --> 00:00:56.841
movies, that’s a stigma, it stigmatizes
you and then most people think that’s all

00:00:56.841 --> 00:01:00.420
that they can do, that’s all that guy knows
how to do.

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:06.640
Let’s just throw a name out there Wes Craven
on an obviously way bigger level.

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:08.860
He struggled with that his entire career.

00:01:08.860 --> 00:01:11.560
That’s why he made the movie with Meryl
Streep [1999 - MUSIC OF THE HEART] because

00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:14.709
he was tired of making horror, he wanted to
make other things.

00:01:14.709 --> 00:01:18.140
Most horror directors want to make other types
of movies, they just don’t get to.

00:01:18.140 --> 00:01:22.470
George Romero had plenty of things he wanted
to do, Wes Craven…they all do…John Carpenter…all

00:01:22.470 --> 00:01:23.470
of them.

00:01:23.470 --> 00:01:27.060
But they are generally not going to get it
and I’m talking about major people here,

00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:31.909
they are not going to get it because their
track record says “he makes this type of

00:01:31.909 --> 00:01:33.490
film.”

00:01:33.490 --> 00:01:38.329
My track record says I make these types of
films and I don’t think that Netflix would

00:01:38.329 --> 00:01:39.329
care about that.

00:01:39.329 --> 00:01:40.770
I think they would see that as a negative.

00:01:40.770 --> 00:01:43.189
Maybe if I’d only made one short [film]
they might be interested in having a meeting

00:01:43.189 --> 00:01:51.039
with me, a short that was very hot let’s
just say that played at some major festival.

00:01:51.039 --> 00:01:54.420
And that’s not because I don’t have any
ideas for things or I don’t have ideas that

00:01:54.420 --> 00:01:59.219
could be expanded to TV series so yeah…

00:01:59.219 --> 00:02:09.009
Film Courage: So having been here in the 1990’s
and then entering a new era where there is

00:02:09.009 --> 00:02:14.450
going to be younger people in charge, do you
find you have a harder time telling you no

00:02:14.450 --> 00:02:22.120
than Get Xer’s or people in another decade
where it seems like it was okay to be a little

00:02:22.120 --> 00:02:23.120
more blunt?

00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:26.129
Now we are very sensitive and we’re dancing
around everybody’s feelings and nobody wants

00:02:26.129 --> 00:02:27.569
to tell anybody no.

00:02:27.569 --> 00:02:34.480
But then they are not telling you the truth
because…and I’m seeing a lot of that and

00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:38.910
maybe it’s just better for people to tell
you no.

00:02:38.910 --> 00:02:44.250
Brad: Yeah, there is…it’s because of so
much online communication there is the classic

00:02:44.250 --> 00:02:51.709
no response, sort of the ghosting or whatever
that people do where they just want to move

00:02:51.709 --> 00:02:54.909
away from the situation or say no or whatever,
they just won’t do anything.

00:02:54.909 --> 00:02:59.340
I guess like no response is okay whatever
that is.

00:02:59.340 --> 00:03:06.140
I don’t know I mean we are pretty straightforward
with people like we are very straightforward

00:03:06.140 --> 00:03:07.140
in auditions.

00:03:07.140 --> 00:03:13.040
We are very straightforward in meetings about
when we’re interviewing crew that doesn’t

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:15.860
mean that things are not going to still come
up later.

00:03:15.860 --> 00:03:20.040
Sometimes it doesn’t matter how much you
talk until you are blue in the face and impress

00:03:20.040 --> 00:03:25.030
something upon someone they either aren’t
listening to you or they’ve got an agenda

00:03:25.030 --> 00:03:29.409
or they just decide that they’ve got another
idea later on.

00:03:29.409 --> 00:03:30.409
That can all happen.

00:03:30.409 --> 00:03:33.389
You have to be prepared for that.

00:03:33.389 --> 00:03:37.030
We just try to be as straightforward as possible
and what other people do we can’t really

00:03:37.030 --> 00:03:38.030
say.

00:03:38.030 --> 00:03:42.480
But if we get a bad vibe off of somebody or
a non-committal vibe or whatever, we just

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:44.170
move on.

00:03:44.170 --> 00:03:51.170
There is a lot of moving on you do in this
business…a ton of it, too much.

00:03:51.170 --> 00:03:54.420
And sometimes it happens way quicker than
you think it will.

00:03:54.420 --> 00:03:59.379
But that is something else you need to get
used to if you’re going to have a career

00:03:59.379 --> 00:04:10.879
on any level is just get used to change and
new faces and kind of self-starting and coming

00:04:10.879 --> 00:04:15.920
up with things almost every film is your first
film I’m sure you’ve heard that before.

00:04:15.920 --> 00:04:21.039
I feel like I’ve felt that way many times
that I’m trying to pitch something to some

00:04:21.039 --> 00:04:23.970
people and I’m going why do I even have
to do this?

00:04:23.970 --> 00:04:30.530
I’ve got over 20 features…because…because
that is just how this business is.

00:04:30.530 --> 00:04:36.810
People always want, you’ve always got to
prove yourself to somebody in some way, some

00:04:36.810 --> 00:04:38.720
more than others but.

00:04:38.720 --> 00:04:44.039
Film Courage: Knowing that does it make it
less stressful?

00:04:44.039 --> 00:04:48.470
Knowing that you have to self-start again
and then you go okay I am back here again.

00:04:48.470 --> 00:04:50.990
Brad: It’s annoying I mean I’ll be honest
with you, it’s annoying.

00:04:50.990 --> 00:04:57.901
Of course because you know there are other
people who don’t have to do that, there

00:04:57.901 --> 00:05:01.259
are I mean on a higher tier there are people
who don’t have to do that.

00:05:01.259 --> 00:05:08.520
But there are an alarming number of accomplished
people who do so that makes it a little easier

00:05:08.520 --> 00:05:14.610
to take I guess when you know that some of
your heroes still have to put on a dog and

00:05:14.610 --> 00:05:18.340
pony show with investors or whatever.

00:05:18.340 --> 00:05:28.840
I don’t know I mean we try to take things,
we try to be as calm as we can over time.

00:05:28.840 --> 00:05:33.710
That’s one thing that Josephina [Sykes]
try to sort of learn with some of these experiences

00:05:33.710 --> 00:05:37.379
that you can get very heated over these little
situations sometimes with film.

00:05:37.379 --> 00:05:41.880
That’s probably something I would tell anybody
really is a lot of times it’s not a big

00:05:41.880 --> 00:05:43.620
a deal as you think it is.

00:05:43.620 --> 00:05:49.410
If it’s an editing issue, if it’s a sound
issue, if it’s a certain shot you don’t…believe

00:05:49.410 --> 00:05:55.199
me, it’s not as big a deal as you think
it is.

00:05:55.199 --> 00:05:58.310
Sometimes you can get very fixated on these
types of things and you can get very stressed

00:05:58.310 --> 00:05:59.310
about this stuff.

00:05:59.310 --> 00:06:00.460
It’s not even about personality.

00:06:00.460 --> 00:06:03.050
Sometimes it’s technical things too.

00:06:03.050 --> 00:06:08.280
You just try to be as calm as you can on those
types of situations.

00:06:08.280 --> 00:06:09.680
Questions For The Viewers: Do you think having
too many credits can hurt you in the film

00:06:09.680 --> 00:06:09.690
industry?

